# Feature tests in apps/web

Test **what the user can do**, not how the screen looks. A feature test answers a capability question (`Can the user see gardens?`). A unit test protects logic that capability needs (`createGardenSchema` rejects invalid input).

Do not add visual, layout, copy-structure, or component-snapshot tests.

Follow the [testing trophy](https://kentcdodds.com/blog/the-testing-trophy-and-testing-classifications): most effort on integration tests, fewer unit tests, a thin e2e layer. A page that wires several components is still an **integration** test, not e2e.

## Where a capability test lives

| Kind | Use when | Place |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Integration (default) | The capability is one page/flow (see gardens, add a garden). Stub HTTP. | `apps/web/tests/routes/*.spec.tsx` |
| Unit | Isolated logic the feature depends on (Zod schema, copy mapper). No render. | `apps/web/tests/**/*.spec.ts` |
| E2E | The capability needs a real document/browser (MPA navigation, loader HTML). Keep this set small. | `apps/web-e2e` |

Name tests as capabilities: `user can see gardens`, `user can add a garden`.

## Integration tests

- Use stubbed `fetch` (same helpers as the gardens spec). Never hit the live API from Vitest (delay + ~10% errors).
- Assert **outcomes**: garden/plant data on the page, request URL/body, list contents after a mutation.
- Failed load: the user is told **why** gardens are not shown, can retry, then sees gardens. Keep `retry: false` in the test QueryClient so the first failure is what the user sees.
- Failed create: the garden is not added.
- Do not assert column headers, skeleton markup, `data-required`, modal chrome, CSS, or exact layout.

## Unit tests (safeguards)

Add a unit test only for non-trivial logic a feature test would bury: Zod schemas that gate a mutation, extracted error-status → copy mappers, query-key invariants (`plantQueryKeys.byGarden` from `plants.const.ts` scoped by `gardenId`).

Do not unit-test presentational components, `api()` plumbing, or React Query wiring the integration test already exercises.

## E2E

Only for capabilities that integration tests cannot prove. Do not point Playwright at the live slow/random API plugins.

## Agent do / don't

**Do**

- Add an integration capability test when the user can do something new on a page
- Unit-test the schema/mapper that feature needs, without rendering
- Use Playwright only when the flow needs a real document (cross-page MPA, loader HTML)

**Don't**

- Treat “spans several components” as a reason to write Playwright; that is still a route integration test
- Test how the UI looks (headers, skeletons, labels, spacing)
- Add a `GardenList` / modal test as a stand-in for the capability
- Copy layout assertions from older specs
- Call the real API from Vitest

## Good / bad

```ts
test('user can see gardens', async () => {
  stubGardensApi({ list: () => jsonResponse([garden]) });
  renderGardensPage();
  expect(await screen.findByText('Front yard')).toBeTruthy();
});

test('user is told why gardens are missing and can retry', async () => {
  let shouldFail = true;
  stubGardensApi({
    list: () => {
      if (shouldFail) {
        shouldFail = false;
        return new Response('boom', { status: 500 });
      }
      return jsonResponse([garden]);
    },
  });
  renderGardensPage();
  expect(await screen.findByText("Couldn't load gardens")).toBeTruthy();
  fireEvent.click(screen.getByRole('button', { name: 'Try again' }));
  expect(await screen.findByText('Front yard')).toBeTruthy();
});

test('createGardenSchema requires a name', () => {
  const result = createGardenSchema.safeParse({
    gardenName: '',
    totalSurfaceArea: 10,
  });
  expect(result.success).toBe(false);
});
```

```ts
test('renders garden table headers', () => {
  expect(screen.getByRole('columnheader', { name: 'Garden name' })).toBeTruthy();
});
```
